from a1 import t
C2='Christ and the Trinity'
t('trinity', C2, 'The Trinity', 'The Trinity is not in the Bible; it is a pagan idea or a contradiction.',
 ["Catholics worship one God in three Persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit, equal in being and distinct as Persons. The word “Trinity” is not in the Bible, any more than “Bible” is, but the teaching is. The Old Testament insists that God is one (Deuteronomy 6:4); the New Testament shows the Father, the Son and the Spirit each called God and acting together: at Jesus’ baptism (Matthew 3:16-17), in the baptismal formula “in the name (singular) of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit” (Matthew 28:19), and in Paul’s blessing (2 Corinthians 13:13).",
  "The Church put this in words at the Councils of Nicaea (325) and Constantinople (381) to answer those who denied that the Son is truly God. It is a mystery, which means that we cannot fully understand it, but it is not a contradiction: God is one in nature (what He is) and three in Persons (who He is). It is not saying 1 = 3. The Trinity is also at the heart of how Christians pray: “in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.”"],
 [('Deuteronomy 6:4','The Lord our God is one'),('Matthew 28:19','Baptise in the name of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit'),('John 1:1-3','The Word was with God, and the Word was God'),('2 Corinthians 13:13','The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, the love of God, the fellowship of the Holy Spirit'),('Matthew 3:16-17','Father, Son and Spirit at the Baptism')],
 [232,233,234,253,254,255,261],['Trinity','Holy Spirit','Nicaea'],['divinity-of-christ'])

t('divinity-of-christ', C2, 'Is Jesus God?', 'Jesus never claimed to be God; that was invented later.',
 ["The Gospels show Jesus claiming what only God can claim. He forgives sins (Mark 2:5-7), is Lord of the Sabbath (Mark 2:28), and says “before Abraham was, I AM” (John 8:58), using the divine name from Exodus 3:14; his listeners picked up stones to kill him for blasphemy. He says, “I and the Father are one” (John 10:30), and accepts Thomas’ confession, “My Lord and my God” (John 20:28). John’s Gospel begins, “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was God… and the Word became flesh” (John 1:1, 14), and Paul says that in Christ “the whole fullness of deity dwells bodily” (Colossians 2:9).",
  "The Church teaches that Jesus is one Divine Person with two natures, truly God and truly man, as the Council of Chalcedon (451) put it. His miracles, and above all his resurrection, are the signs given to confirm who he is."],
 [('John 8:56-59','Before Abraham was, I AM'),('John 10:30-33','I and the Father are one'),('John 20:28','My Lord and my God'),('Mark 2:5-12','Who can forgive sins but God alone?'),('Philippians 2:5-11','He was in the form of God'),('Colossians 1:15-20','In him all things were created')],
 [464,465,466,467,468,469],['Jesus','Divinity of Christ','Incarnation'],['trinity','resurrection'])

t('resurrection', C2, 'Did Jesus really rise from the dead?', 'The resurrection is a myth, or the disciples hallucinated.',
 ["The earliest written testimony is Paul’s creed in 1 Corinthians 15:3-8, which he “received” (so it is older than the letter, written about AD 55): Christ died for our sins, was buried, was raised on the third day, and appeared to Cephas, the Twelve, more than five hundred at once (most of whom were still alive), James, and last of all to Paul. The Gospels agree that women found the tomb empty, an unlikely detail to invent in a world where a woman’s testimony counted for little. The disciples, who had run away at the arrest, became fearless witnesses who suffered and died rather than deny what they had seen.",
  "A resurrection is not the resuscitation of a corpse: the risen Jesus has a transformed, glorified body that can pass through closed doors but also eats fish and shows his wounds. It is a historical event with a meaning beyond history, which is why Paul says, “If Christ has not been raised, your faith is futile” (1 Corinthians 15:17). The Catechism calls it “the crowning truth of our faith in Christ.”"],
 [('1 Corinthians 15:3-8','Paul’s creed of the resurrection'),('1 Corinthians 15:12-20','If Christ has not been raised, our faith is futile'),('Luke 24:36-43','Touch me and see; he ate a piece of fish'),('John 20:24-29','Thomas and the wounds'),('Matthew 28:1-10','The empty tomb')],
 [638,639,640,641,642,643,644,645,646,647,648,651],['Resurrection','Easter','Empty tomb'],['divinity-of-christ'])

C3='Mary and the saints'
t('mary-mother-of-god', C3, 'Mother of God', 'Calling Mary the Mother of God means she is older than God, or a goddess.',
 ["“Mother of God” (Theotokos, “God-bearer”) was defined at the Council of Ephesus (431), and it is a statement about Jesus before it is a statement about Mary. Mary is the mother of Jesus, and Jesus is a divine Person. Mary is not the source of his divinity, which is eternal, but she gave him his human nature, and a mother is the mother of a whole person, not of part of one. Elizabeth, filled with the Holy Spirit, called her “the mother of my Lord” (Luke 1:43), and “Lord” is the title the Old Testament uses for God.",
  "To deny the title is to suggest that the child in the womb was not God yet. That is why the Church insisted on it: it guards the truth that Jesus is God made man."],
 [('Luke 1:43','The mother of my Lord'),('Galatians 4:4','God sent his Son, born of a woman'),('Luke 1:30-35','The child will be called the Son of God'),('John 2:1-5','The mother of Jesus at Cana')],
 [495,509,975],['Mary','Theotokos','Ephesus','Mother of God'],['immaculate-conception','intercession-of-saints'])

t('immaculate-conception', C3, 'The Immaculate Conception', 'Mary was a sinner like everyone else; the Immaculate Conception is not in the Bible.',
 ["The Immaculate Conception does not mean that Jesus was conceived without a human father (that is the virginal conception). It means that Mary herself, from the first moment of her existence, was preserved by God from original sin by a grace given in view of the merits of her Son (defined by Pope Pius IX in 1854). She was redeemed in the most perfect way: kept from falling, not lifted out afterward.",
  "The Church finds the root of this in Scripture’s pattern: the angel greets Mary as “full of grace” (Luke 1:28), a title in place of her name, and the Ark of the Covenant, which carried God’s word, was made pure and holy. Eve was without sin before the Fall; Mary, the “new Eve”, was called to be the mother of the Redeemer, and the early Fathers contrasted “Eve’s disobedience” with “Mary’s obedience”. Romans 3:23 (“all have sinned”) allows exceptions in context: Jesus did not sin, and infants and the severely disabled cannot personally sin."],
 [('Luke 1:28','Hail, full of grace'),('Genesis 3:15','Enmity between the woman and the serpent'),('Revelation 12:1','A woman clothed with the sun'),('Luke 1:46-49','He who is mighty has done great things for me')],
 [490,491,492,493,494],['Mary','Immaculate Conception','Original sin'],['mary-mother-of-god','assumption'])

t('assumption', C3, 'The Assumption of Mary', 'The Assumption is not in the Bible and was invented in 1950.',
 ["The Church teaches that Mary, at the end of her earthly life, was taken body and soul into heavenly glory. It was defined in 1950, but the belief is much older: the feast of her “Dormition” (falling asleep) was celebrated in the East by the sixth century, and no city in the ancient world claims her relics, though cities claimed those of many other saints.",
  "Scripture does not describe it, but supports its fittingness. Enoch and Elijah were taken up (Genesis 5:24, 2 Kings 2:11); the Ark of the Covenant, which was God’s dwelling, was brought to rest in the Temple (Psalm 132:8, 2 Chronicles 5:7), and Revelation 11:19-12:1 moves from the Ark appearing in the heavenly Temple to “a woman clothed with the sun”. The Church sees in Mary a preview of what awaits all the faithful: the resurrection of the body."],
 [('Psalm 132:8','Arise, O Lord, and go to your resting place, you and the ark of your might'),('Revelation 11:19-12:1','The ark in heaven, and a woman clothed with the sun'),('1 Corinthians 15:20-23','Christ the first fruits, then those who belong to him'),('2 Kings 2:11','Elijah taken up')],
 [966,974],['Mary','Assumption','Dormition'],['immaculate-conception'])

t('perpetual-virginity', C3, 'Was Mary always a virgin?', 'The Bible speaks of the “brothers” of Jesus, so Mary had other children.',
 ["The Church teaches that Mary was a virgin before, during and after the birth of Jesus (CCC 499). In the Bible the Greek word for brother, adelphos, is used broadly, as the Hebrew “ach” is, for cousins and kinsmen: Lot is called Abraham’s “brother” though he is his nephew (Genesis 14:14, 16). The Gospels name “James and Joses” as sons of another Mary who stood at the cross (Matthew 27:56), not of Mary of Nazareth. At the cross Jesus entrusts his mother to John (John 19:26-27), which would be unnecessary if she had other sons to care for her.",
  "Mary’s question at the Annunciation, “How can this be, since I have no husband?” (Luke 1:34), has long been read as showing a decision to remain a virgin. The Fathers and the Reformers (including Luther and Zwingli) held this teaching."],
 [('Luke 1:34','How can this be, since I have no husband?'),('Matthew 13:55','Are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon and Judas?'),('Matthew 27:56','Mary the mother of James and Joseph'),('John 19:26-27','Behold your mother'),('Genesis 14:14-16','Abraham’s “brother” Lot was his nephew')],
 [499,500,501,502,503,504,505,506],['Mary','Perpetual virginity','Brothers of the Lord'],['mary-mother-of-god'])

t('intercession-of-saints', C3, 'Praying to the saints', 'Praying to saints is worship of the dead, and Jesus is the only mediator.',
 ["Catholics do not worship saints; worship (latria) belongs to God alone. We ask the saints to pray for us, just as we ask living friends to pray for us. Paul asks the Romans to “strive together with me in your prayers” (Romans 15:30), and James says that “the prayer of a righteous person has great power” (James 5:16). The saints in heaven are not dead: God “is not God of the dead, but of the living” (Matthew 22:32), and Jesus speaks with Moses and Elijah at the Transfiguration.",
  "Paul’s “one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus” (1 Timothy 2:5) is spoken in the very passage where he urges prayers for everyone (2:1-4). Christ is the one Mediator of redemption; asking someone to pray with us does not replace his mediation but shares in it, as members of his Body. Revelation shows the saints in heaven offering our prayers to God (Revelation 5:8; 6:9-10). The Church says, “Being more closely united to Christ, those who dwell in heaven fix the whole Church more firmly in holiness” (CCC 956)."],
 [('Revelation 5:8','The prayers of the saints, in golden bowls of incense'),('James 5:16','The prayer of a righteous person has great power'),('Hebrews 12:1','A great cloud of witnesses'),('1 Timothy 2:1-5','One mediator between God and men'),('Luke 9:30-31','Moses and Elijah speak with Jesus')],
 [956,957,958,2683,2684],['Saints','Intercession','Communion of saints'],['mary-mother-of-god','purgatory'])
